1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,119
Episode Sixty-Seven left us with a rule:
name a pattern when a problem recurs.
Creation is one of those problems. Objects
need validation, defaults, shared
instances, or alternate implementations.

2
00:00:13,239 --> 00:00:26,479
Creational patterns manage how objects
come to life. In modern Java services, DI
containers often own lifecycle — classical
factory and singleton moves migrate into
the container. The ideas do not vanish.

3
00:00:26,599 --> 00:00:42,159
Their addresses change. Start with the
singleton everyone meets too early: Look
at this java for a moment. A simple eager
singleton: one instance, private
constructor, global access. Clear — and a
magnet for misuse.

4
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:47,039
Singleton as a global mutable bag
recreates global variables with better
branding.

5
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:57,160
Double-checked locking myths still
circulate for lazy singletons; modern Java
has safer idioms, enums-as-singleton, or
simply letting a container manage scope.

6
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:03,079
The interview trap is debating locking
trivia while missing the design smell: do
you need a global at all?

7
00:01:03,259 --> 00:01:14,379
Separate "one instance" from "global
access." A process may need a single cache
manager without every class reaching into
a static getter. Injection gives you the
single instance without the global lookup.

8
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:25,099
A Spring bean defaulting to singleton
scope is not the same social object as a
mutable App.INSTANCE bag — dependencies
remain injected and testable. Prefer that
model for application services.

9
00:01:25,219 --> 00:01:35,180
Factory methods appear when construction
deserves a name.
Order.createFromCart(cart) encapsulates
invariants better than a public
constructor with eight parameters.

10
00:01:35,299 --> 00:01:44,620
Factories also hide concrete classes
behind interfaces — useful when tests need
fakes. A factory that only wraps new
without policy is ceremony.

11
00:01:44,739 --> 00:01:53,060
Use factories when creation policy exists:
caching, subtype selection, validation, or
environment-specific wiring.

12
00:01:53,179 --> 00:02:02,779
Boundary types shine here:
Money.of(amount, currency) rejects invalid
combinations; UserId.from(string)
encapsulates parsing.

13
00:02:02,899 --> 00:02:16,859
Builders earn their keep when telescoping
constructors appear and call sites become
unreadable: Look at this java for a
moment. An email message needs from, to,
subject, optional cc, bcc, attachments,
headers.

14
00:02:16,979 --> 00:02:27,979
Telescoping constructors force nulls and
argument-order bugs. A builder makes the
call site self-describing and can enforce
invariants in build() — requiring at least
one recipient.

15
00:02:28,099 --> 00:02:42,139
That is creational policy, not mere sugar.
Prototype — cloning — shows up rarely in
Java application code; prefer clear copy
constructors or factories unless you truly
need clone-style duplication.

16
00:02:42,259 --> 00:02:53,939
In Spring and similar containers, beans
have scopes and lifecycle callbacks. The
container may be your factory. Constructor
injection makes dependencies honest
instead of reaching for App.INSTANCE.

17
00:02:54,060 --> 00:03:05,860
You still decide what is singleton-scoped,
what is request-scoped, what should be
built per call, and what a builder should
assemble before registration. Walk a
failure mode.

18
00:03:05,979 --> 00:03:14,339
A team makes a "service locator" singleton
that hands out dependencies anywhere.
Tests become hard. Circular creation
appears.

19
00:03:14,459 --> 00:03:23,339
The creational pattern that would have
helped was not a fancier singleton — it
was explicit construction or DI with
constructor injection.

20
00:03:23,459 --> 00:03:34,939
If construction is dishonest — half-
initialized objects, setters required
after new — every creational pattern
becomes makeup on a broken type. Fix the
type's birth certificate first.

21
00:03:35,059 --> 00:03:42,379
In tests: if you cannot construct a valid
domain object in one or two lines,
construction policy is too scattered.

22
00:03:42,499 --> 00:03:49,099
If asked builder versus telescoping
constructors, say: builder keeps call
sites readable when many optional fields
exist.

23
00:03:49,219 --> 00:03:58,299
If asked about singletons, admit caveats:
global mutable state, testing pain, and
that containers often own the one-instance
story now.

24
00:03:58,419 --> 00:04:06,259
On double-checked locking: say why people
reached for it historically, why it was
easy to get wrong, and what you would do
now instead.

25
00:04:06,379 --> 00:04:17,699
Creation sets the stage for structure:
once objects exist, how do we assemble
them so behavior can grow without subclass
explosions — adapters, decorators,
facades, and proxies?
